I researched it, not sure whether the process is completely correct, the conclusion is as follows:
It is not operable to specify parameters through a similar `kubectl run --override`

The debug command is `kubectl debug node/nodeName -it --image=busybox`,the debugpod yaml generated by this command is(only show continer key information)
```
{
	"spec": {
		"containers": [{
			"name": "debugger",
			"image": "10.7.106.189/daocloud/busybox",
			"resources": {},
			"volumeMounts": [{
				"name": "host-root",
				"mountPath": "/host"
			}, {
				"name": "default-token-lwjgm",
				"readOnly": true,
				"mountPath": "/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount"
			}],
			"terminationMessagePath": "/dev/termination-log",
			"terminationMessagePolicy": "File",
			"imagePullPolicy": "Always",
			"stdin": true,
			"tty": true
		}]
	}
}
```

If we override debugPod yaml through `overrides`
My test yaml is `{"spec":{"containers":[{"securityContext":{"capabilities":{"add":["NET_ADMIN","SYS_TIME"]}}}]}}`

But `jsonpatch.MergePatch` cannot merge as we expected, the actual result is

```
{
	"spec": {
		"containers": [{
			"securityContext": {
				"capabilities": {
					"add": ["NET_ADMIN", "SYS_TIME"]
				}
			}
		}]
	}
}
```

The merge of the array cannot be completed well, and the configuration other than the debugPod securityContext has been overwritten, which does not match expectations.

At the same time, many configurations of the debugPod container are dynamically obtained at runtime, such as `volumeMounts`,`container-name`. The user does not know these values ​​when using `--overrides`

The result of kubectl run execution pod yaml is relatively clear, so it is more friendly to pass `override`, but `kubectl debug` seems to be different

Not sure if my test steps are completely correct

